titre$ Pluteus à crochet_ Justo
com$ Adapt P Bertea

1,0 $ Lamellar edge distinctly brown to dark brown over its whole length. $ P. atromarginatus
1,2 $ Lamellar edge white or pink

2,3 $ Clamp-connections present on pileipellis hyphae
2,7 $ Clamp-connections absent on pileipellis hyphae

3,4 $ On conifer wood
3,5 $ On angiosperm wood or terrestrial

4,0 $ Basidiospores 6.0–8.6(–9.5) × (4.0–)4.5–5.7(–6.2) µm, avl × avw = 6.8–7.5 × 4.8–5.2 µm. Cheilocystidia 20–70 × 10–25(–30) µm, predominantly clavate, narrowly clavate or spheropedunculate.$ P. pouzarianus
4,0 $ Basidiospores 7.0–9.5 × (4.5–)5.0–7.0 µm, avl × avw = 7.8–8.4 × 5.6–6.3 µm. Cheilocystidia 55–120(–200) × 8–25(–30) µm, predominantly narrowly clavate or cylindrical.$ P. primus (see also P. kovalenkoi)

5,0 $  Pileus predominantly gray. Blue or blue-green tinges usually present on pileus and/or stipe. Basidiospores (avl × avw) 8.3–9.5 × 6.2–6.8 µm.$ P. salicinus
5,6 $ Pileus predominantly white or brown. Without blue or blue-green tinges on the basidiocarp. Basidiospores (avl × avw) 5.8–8.1 × 4.3–6.2 µm

6,0 $ Pileus usually white, sometimes pale brown around center. Basidiospores 5.0–7.5(–8.0) × 3.5–5.0(–5.5) µm, avl × avw = 5.8–6.5 × 4.3–4.6 µm $ P. pellitus
6,0 $ Pileus brown to dark brown all over. Basidiospores 6.0–9.6(–10.5) × 4.5–7.1µm, avl × avw = 7.1–8.1 × 4.8–6.2 µm.$ P. alniphilus and P. brunneidiscus (check the respective descriptions; see also P. kovalenkoi)
7,8 $  Intermediate cystidia predominantly fusiform and non-hooked
7,10 $ Intermediate cystidia variable, without a predominant morphological type

8,0 $ Basidiospores 5.5–8.0 × 3.5–5.5 µm, avl × avw = 6.1–7.0 × 4.2–4.8 µm. Cheilocystidia usually not forming a well-developed and continuous strip. In parks, urban areas and temperate forests $ P. petasatus
8,9 $ Basidiospores 5.5–8.5 × 4.5–6.5(–7.0) µm, avl × avw = 6.8–7.6 × 5.0–5.8 µm. Cheilocystidia usually forming a well-developed and continuous strip. In boreal or transitional boreal/temperate forests

9,0 $ Pileus white, usually with well defined squamules or radial fibrils. Only recorded on Betula and Alnus $ P. leucoborealis
9,0 $ Pileus dark brown or gray-brown, innately radially fibrillose but without superficial squamules or fibrils. On various angiosperms (including Betula).$ P. rangifer

10,0 $ Stipe usually with conspicuous longitudinal fibrils and/or squamules. Hooks on the pleurocystidia predominantly entire $ P. cervinus (if collected in boreal areas check also P. rangifer)
10,0 $ Stipe usually without conspicuous longitudinal fibrils and/or squamules. Hooks on the pleurocystidia predominantly bifid $ P. hongoi

